```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:

Affected component: The static default route in router6's kernel routing
table and FRR RIB.

Mechanism: Router6 reaches Location-B hosts (172.18.50.0/24 and
172.18.51.0/24) via directly-connected interfaces, and reaches other
topology subnets via RIP-learned specifics sourced from router3
(10.50.1.1). However, for any destination that does not match one of
those explicit prefixes — any external or truly unknown address — the
kernel requires a default route (0.0.0.0/0) as a catch-all gateway.
With no default present, `ip route get 8.8.8.8` returns "Network is
unreachable" immediately at the kernel level; the packet is dropped
before it can even leave the router.

Localization: The fault is entirely on router6. Its startup script
(router6.startup) installs the line `ip route add default via 10.50.1.1
metric 150` (translated from the Cisco `ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0
10.50.1.1 150`). The current kernel routing table has no 0.0.0.0/0
entry; `show ip route` in FRR likewise shows no static or RIP-learned
default. Router3 (the upstream peer at 10.50.1.1) does not originate a
default into RIP, so no recovery via RIP is possible.

Root cause: The static default route `0.0.0.0/0 via 10.50.1.1 metric
150` was deleted from router6 after the lab started, leaving the router
with no path of last resort.

Corrective fix: Re-install the default route on router6:
`ip route add default via 10.50.1.1 metric 150`
(or in vtysh: `ip route 0.0.0.0/0 10.50.1.1 150`). To make it
persistent across restarts, add the equivalent `ip route 0.0.0.0/0
10.50.1.1 150` stanza back into the FRR staticd configuration.
=== END ===
```